> > > >> to unmarked
> > > >> Hi team,
> > > >>
> > > >> First of all, thank you for this package and your continuous support.
> > > >>
> > > >> My question relates to an analysis of a long-term fish observation
> > > >> database which I'm considering publishing. I am after a sense check of my
> > > >> conclusions/assumptions before I commit to preparing a manuscript- one
> > > >> probably aimed at a modest journal (~IF 2) and maybe in the form of a short
> > > >> communication.
> > > >>
> > > >> I have database that includes 42 years of data collected over the
> > > >> management area of a large city that has 5 major drainage areas. Think of
> > > >> it a bit like a citizen science database. Sampling was not spatially or
> > > >> temporally consistent across sites within river basins, so I've split the
> > > >> river basins up into sub-catchment areas which act as my 'sites' and I've
> > > >> combined all observations within each site for each year. To reduce the
> > > >> amount of missing data, my sites are fairly large and relatively few (32
> > > >> sites total, see figure below for example). In the dynamic occupancy model
> > > >> I am using each year of data as secondary surveys (replicates) and three
> > > >> years are combined to make one primary sampling occasion (14 primary
> > > >> sampling occasions over 42 years).
> > > >> [image: Study sites map.jpg]
> > > >> The single species I'm interested is migratory, swimming to and from the
> > > >> ocean each year. Historically, barriers to migration have impeded their
> > > >> upstream migration at several locations, restricting their distribution.
> > > >> Efforts have been made to remove these barriers over the last couple of
> > > >> decades though and the expectation is that these species have benefited
> > > >> through an increase in distribution. My aim is to test this assumption.
> > > >>
> > > >> The dataset is imperfect, so I've kept the model simple with one
> > > >> explanatory variable that describes the proportion of a catchment (at the
> > > >> point where a site is) that is unimpeded by barriers in each year. As we
> > > >> move towards the present, this proportion has increased.
> > > >>
> > > >> The results show that the distribution of the target species has more
> > > >> than doubled over the study period. The CIs don't overlap between the first
> > > >> and last seasons, indicating that the change is significant. Large jumps in
> > > >> occupancy are observed when various barriers are removed. Furthermore,
> > > >> colonisation is significantly correlated with the 'barrier removal'
> > > >> variable. I've compared the model versus a null model and other variations
> > > >> to show that it describes the data best. For the best model P-hat = 0.96.
> > > >> chi-squ = 0.083.
> > > >>
> > > >> The results fit expectations, are significant, and the model passes the
> > > >> model fit test. That said, I'm working with 32 sites and a fair bit of
> > > >> missing data and the power to detect change is low. I had a play around
> > > >> with the power analysis presented in Guillera‐Arroita, G., &
> > > >> Lahoz‐Monfort, J. J. (2012), which suggested I'd need 2-3x more sites to
> > > >> achieve power of 0.8. I think the CIs from the occupancy analysis might be
> > > >> a reasonable guide as to the power of the analysis given my actual dataset
> > > >> though?
> > > >>
> > > >> *I was hoping you could provide your general take on the validity of the
> > > >> findings. Does the poor site replication mean that the results are
> > > >> effectively unreliable, or do the various tests of significance and model
> > > >> fit indicate that it is reliable enough? *
> > > >>
> > > >> The whole thing is rough around the edges, but I think it could be a
> > > >> worthwhile example of the benefit of mining a historic dataset to assess
> > > >> the impact of waterway management efforts that have been taking place over
> > > >> far longer time periods than any standardised monitoring programs capture.
> > > >>
> > > >> *## This is the model used*
> > > >> dynamic_occ_m1 <- colext(
> > > >> # Psi depends on initial proportion of catchment available
> > > >> psiformula = ~Barrier1,
> > > >> # colonization depends on change the proportion of catchment available
> > > >> gammaformula = ~barriers_time,
> > > >> # extinction depends on the proportion of catchment available
> > > >> epsilonformula = ~barriers_time ,
> > > >> # Detection varies between years
> > > >> pformula = ~year,
> > > >> #
> > > >> data = Tupong_unmarkedMultFrame,
> > > >> method = "BFGS")
> > > >>
> > > >> Thanks in advance for your time. It's much appreciated.
> > > >>
> > > >> Regards,
> > > >> James
