Hi Ken,

I have continued working on this today and have a follow up question. I have created the simulations and now want to use the predict() function to make sure everything is behaving correctly. However, when I use the code below I get the error "Error in attr(x,"formula") %||%{ : invalid formula. I check the documentation to make sure type should be phi and that seems correct. Can you explain where I've gone wrong?

 betapredict <- unmarked::predict(tempXpsiphiXbeta, type = "phi", newdata = temp, appendData = T)

-Reed

On Tue, Feb 4, 2025 at 8:44 PM Reed Scott <rscot...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hi Ken,
>
> Thanks for the explanation. That makes a lot of sense and actually helps me a lot! I can do work on a smaller simulated dataset. I will have to consider how I want to approach the fact that it won't be able to handle as many tests as I have, but that might be a better question for the HMEcology listserv.
>
> To clarify, visits aren't really considered in the structure of the data; they're just used to determine how many frogs (and how many tests) are collected total across the season. If I wanted to, I could consider them as part of the model and do a dynamic multiscale occupancy model, but that's not something I'm interested in at the moment.
>
> Thanks again Ken, I really appreciate your help.
> -Reed
>
> On Tue, Feb 4, 2025 at 8:26 PM Ken Kellner <con...@kenkellner.com> wrote:
> > Hi Reed,
> >
> > The way goccu calculates the likelihood requires a list of all the possible true availability states for a given site. So for example if you have two primary periods, the possible availability states are 0-0 (if the species was not available in either primary period), 1-0 (avialable for first and not for second), 0-1, 1-1. If you have three periods it's 0-0-0, 1-0-0, and so on.
> >
> > There are always 2^T states where T is the number of primary periods. In your code you specified 36 primary periods which is 2^36 = something like 6.8 x 10^10 possible states. R can't handle this. This implies there is a practical upper limit to the number of primary periods goccu can handle (much like there is a practical limit to the number of species occuMulti can handle).
> >
> > There may be some other way to calculate the likelihood that avoids this issue, but I'm not aware of it. Maybe someone on the listserv knows. Fitting the model in a Bayesian framework might work.
> >
> > Looking at your simulation setup it seems like you actually have three levels of repeated samples within a site: frogs, visits, and tests. I'm not sure what these represent exactly, but I think this is one more level of organization than goccu can handle. It can only handle two levels: primary periods, and secondary periods within primary periods.
> >
> > Ken
> >
> > On Tue, Feb 04, 2025 at 07:53:34PM -0500, Reed Scott wrote:
> > > Hi Ken,
> > >
> > > Thanks for getting back to me! I fixed that part of the code but now I run
> > > in to this error "Cannot allocate vector of size 256.0 gb". I guessed that
> > > this has to do with the large number of detections (108), so I reduced the
> > > number of simulated detections to 54 and that seems to work. However, the
> > > dimensions are based on my real world data so I'd like to simulate a
> > > similar dataset if possible. Do you have any ideas on how to correct this
> > > issue?
> > >
> > > -Reed
> > >
> > > On Tue, Feb 4, 2025 at 6:31 PM Ken Kellner <con...@kenkellner.com> wrote:
> > >
> > > > Hi Reed,
> > > >
> > > > You need to provide the same arguments to simulate() that you would
> > > > provide to goccu() when actually fitting the model. So in this case instead
> > > > of 'formula' you'd provide separate 'psiformula' , 'phiformula', and
> > > > 'pformula' arguments to simulate (see ?goccu).
> > > >
> > > > Ken
> > > >
> > > > On Tue, Feb 04, 2025 at 05:27:09PM -0500, Reed Scott wrote:
> > > > > Hi all,
> > > > >
> > > > > I have run into a bit of a snag while trying to simulate data to consider
> > > > > my analysis + hypotheses for a project. Specifically, I would like to
> > > > > assess data on amphibian diseases using a single season multi-scale (or
> > > > > multi-method) occupancy model. I have 24 sites that I visited 3 times in
> > > > a
> > > > > season; during each visit I collected skin swabs from ~12 individuals and
> > > > > each swab is tested in triplicate in the lab. In this case we are
> > > > treating
> > > > > each test as analogous to a survey, and each individual could be thought
> > > > of
> > > > > as the "subsite". Thus my simulated data should be a 24*108 matrix.
> > > > >
> > > > > I have followed the documentation on the simulate() function, but instead
> > > > > of using occu() I am using goccu(). I want to simulate an effect of
> > > > > temperature on occupancy. However, when I tryo to run the simulate()
> > > > > function I get the error argument "psiformula" is missing, with no
> > > > default.I'm
> > > > > guessing this error is because I am not specifying the formula correctly,
> > > > > but I haven't seen any documentation on how to use simulate() and goccu()
> > > > > together. I would appreciate any help clarifying this issue, and have
> > > > > copied my current code below.
> > > > >
> > > > > library(unmarked)
> > > > > set.seed(1234)
> > > > > ponds <- 24 #number of sites
> > > > > frogs <- 12 #number of frogs PER VISIT
> > > > > visits <- 3 #number of visits to each site
> > > > > tests <- 3 #number of tests per frog
> > > > > total_frogs <- frogs*visits
> > > > > total_tests <- frogs*visits*tests #total number of tests for each pond
> > > > for
> > > > > the season
> > > > >
> > > > > # Create an empty dataframe for the detection data
> > > > > det <- matrix(NA, nrow = ponds, ncol = total_tests)
> > > > >
> > > > > # Create a site lvl covar
> > > > > site_covs <- data.frame(temp = rnorm(ponds))
> > > > >
> > > > > umf <- unmarkedFrameGOccu(y = det, siteCovs = site_covs, numPrimary =
> > > > > total_frogs)
> > > > > head(umf)
> > > > > formula <- ~1~1~temp
> > > > > simulate(umf, model = goccu, formula = formula)
